---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="About" path="/about" description="What Living Stories Sermons are, where they came from, and the traditions they draw on.">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap split wide-left">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
        <h1>Every voice helps tell the story</h1>
        <p class="lead">Living Stories is a way of preaching that turns the sermon into a shared conversation. It grew out of St. Gregory of Nyssa Episcopal Church in San Francisco and follows the Revised Common Lectionary, so it fits into an ordinary Sunday liturgy.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="photo photo-tall">
        <img src="/img/facilitating.webp" alt="A vested preacher seated among a congregation, leading a Living Stories conversation" style="object-position:62% 50%" />
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section band">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">Where it came from</p>
        <h2>It started in a parking lot</h2>
        <p>During the pandemic, as St. Gregory's moved toward regathering, children in the congregation asked to do Godly Play together in person instead of on Zoom. That request became an experiment: Godly Play and Eucharist, held outdoors in the church's back parking lot.</p>
        <p>Over time, the tactile storytelling of Godly Play and the shared participation of the Eucharist grew into a new way of preaching. The whole congregation was invited into the storytelling and the interpretation, and the model took shape inside the Sunday liturgy.</p>
        <p>Today congregations across the country use Living Stories Sermons, and the practice keeps growing through workshops, webinars, and partnerships.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="photo">
        <img src="/img/parking-lot.webp" alt="An outdoor service with children and adults gathered around a table" width="480" height="360" />
        <figcaption>Godly Play and Eucharist in St. Gregory's parking lot</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">What it draws on</p>
        <h2>Godly Play and Montessori, adapted for Sunday worship</h2>
        <p>Living Stories borrows Godly Play's commitment to storytelling, wondering, and tactile materials, and the Montessori trust that people learn best through hands-on exploration. It then adapts those practices for the whole congregation at worship.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid-3" style="margin-top:2rem">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>For all ages</h3>
          <p>Godly Play is usually a children's classroom. Living Stories includes everyone from children to elders, together.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Inside the liturgy</h3>
          <p>It replaces the sermon in the regular service and follows the lectionary, so it needs very little change to the rest of worship.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Interpreted together</h3>
          <p>Wondering becomes a communal act. The preacher facilitates and trusts the Spirit to speak through the voices of the congregation.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section band">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Where it's used</p>
        <h2>A growing network</h2>
        <p>Congregations from coast to coast preach with Living Stories, and their preachers support each other through webinars and a shared newsletter.</p>
        <div class="actions"><a class="btn" href="/get-started">Join them</a></div>
      </div>
      <figure class="photo">
        <img src="/img/map.webp" alt="A map of the United States marking where congregations use Living Stories Sermons" width="1400" height="933" />
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
